\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Interestingly, the method used to generate this graph is also a randomised algorithm -- the method of sampling. We used the idea of law of large numbers by saying that the number of failures divided by total number of runs (10000) should be a good approximation to the failure probability. Of course, 10000 was chosen because it was large as compared to the $n$ under consideration.}}{66}{figure.caption.55}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:randcheck}{{7.1}{66}{Interestingly, the method used to generate this graph is also a randomised algorithm -- the method of sampling. We used the idea of law of large numbers by saying that the number of failures divided by total number of runs (10000) should be a good approximation to the failure probability. Of course, 10000 was chosen because it was large as compared to the $n$ under consideration}{figure.caption.55}{}}
